\subsection{Why square-root cancellation}

The corner count $\high(N)$ is a lattice-point count in a triangle cut out of a conic.
Expanding the indicator of the triangle by Fourier inversion on $\mathbb{Z}/N\mathbb{Z}$ turns
$\high(N)$ into a main term (the area fraction times $C(N)$) plus incomplete character sums
over the conic; such sums are of Kloosterman/Sali\'e type, for which Weil's bound gives
square-root cancellation. This is Conjecture~\ref{conj:equi} below.

\section{Discussion}

Three separate mechanisms seal $H$.

\emph{(i) The bulk is separable.} $H(N)=C(N)/8+O(\sqrt N)$, and $C$ is the classified object
of Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}; the dominant term did not cross the boundary.

\emph{(ii) The low-order bits are separable.} By Theorem~\ref{thm:parity} the parity of $H$ is
the local diagonal count $D(N)\bmod 2$.

\emph{(iii) The residue is at the noise floor.} The non-separable part $\varepsilon$ is real
but $O(\sqrt N)$, structureless in the trace coordinates, and computable only at $\Theta(N)$
cost.

The third mechanism is the general one, and it does \emph{not} derive from separability, which
was deliberately broken (Theorem~\ref{thm:nonsep}). It derives from \emph{aggregation}:
$H(N)$ is a sum of $C(N)\approx N$ indicator values, so it fluctuates by $O(\sqrt N)$ about a
mean dictated by smooth geometry. To obtain accessible factorisation information from a
modular object one must therefore either avoid aggregation (read out an individual point
rather than a count), amplify the $O(\sqrt N)$ signal coherently across related moduli, or
change the computational model so that the aggregation itself becomes cheap. The half-plane
construction advances none of these.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (circle enumeration; $\Theta(N)$ time and space).}
Build a table mapping each residue $r$ to $\{y : y^2\equiv r\}$, then for each $x$ look up
$1-x^2$ and emit all pairs; this yields $C(N)$, $H(N)$ and $\high(N)$ in one pass.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (separable evaluation; $O(\omega(N))$ operations given the
factorisation).} Return $\prod p^{v_p-1}\bigl(p-\chi_p(-1)\bigr)$ with $\chi_p(-1)$ read off
from $p\bmod 4$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (Blum recovery; $O(1)$ given $C(N)$).}
Set $s=C(N)-N-1$ and $d=\sqrt{s^2-4N}$; output $\bigl((s-d)/2,\ (s+d)/2\bigr)$.

\section{Conjectures and future work}

\begin{conjecture}[Equidistribution: the true density is $1/8$]\label{conj:equi}
For odd squarefree $N$, $\;\bigl|8H(N)-C(N)\bigr|=O\bigl(N^{1/2+\epsilon}\bigr)$.
\end{conjecture}

The corner count is a lattice-point count in a triangle cut out of the circle, so the error
term should be governed by incomplete Kloosterman/Sali\'e sums attached to the conic, and
Weil's bound gives square-root cancellation, matching the observed
$\varepsilon\in[-88,+128]$ at $\sqrt N\approx 239$. With the separable side closed in product
form, the only remaining unknown in $8H=C+(8\,\high-C)+8S$ is a pure exponential-sum estimate.

\begin{conjecture}[Higher congruence obstructions]
For odd $N$, $4\mid C(N)$ and more precisely $C(N)\equiv 2S(N)\pmod 4$; and the parity theorem
lifts to
\[
  H(N)\equiv D(N)+2\cdot(\text{number of orbits of the reflection group of size }4)\pmod 4 .
\]
\end{conjecture}

Both are orbit-counting statements for the dihedral group of order $8$ acting on the circle;
the fixed-point strata are exactly the axis points ($S(N)$ of them) and the diagonal points
($D(N)$ of them), already isolated above as separable objects, so what remains is Burnside
bookkeeping.

\begin{conjecture}[The even part]
The prime-power formula and the closed product form are established for odd $N$
(Theorems~\ref{thm:ppow}--\ref{thm:closed}); the open follow-up is $C(2^k)$ and the
interaction of the half-plane cut with the prime $2$. Empirically $C(2^k)=2^{k+1}$ for
$k\ge3$ (e.g.\ $C(8)=16$, $C(16)=32$), reflecting the degeneration of the conic at $2$.
\end{conjecture}

Beyond these: a proof that any $\Theta(N)$-aggregation statistic of the modular circle is
factor-blind at the accessible scale; hint-amplification schemes correlating $\varepsilon$
across families of related moduli; and readouts in models where aggregation is not
$\Theta(N)$.

\section{Conclusion}

Conditioning the modular circle on a non-congruential cut does exactly what it was designed to
do: the resulting half-plane count is provably not a product of local factors. But the count
is then sealed from three directions --- a separable dominant term $C(N)/8$ in closed form, a
purely local parity, and a factor-sensitive yet structureless remainder of size $O(\sqrt N)$
obtainable only at $\Theta(N)$ cost. The classification boundary is real, and crossing it is
easy; escaping aggregation is the hard part, and the crossing does not touch it.

